What is SonarQube?

SonarQube is a code quality and security analysis tool.

It scans your source code and identifies problems such as:

🐛 Bugs
🔐 Security vulnerabilities
⚠️ Code smells
🔄 Duplicate code
📊 Code coverage
🧹 Poor coding practices
🚨 Security hotspots

The main purpose is to find problems before the application is deployed.


What is Nexus?

Nexus Repository is an artifact/repository manager.

Its job is to store and manage the files/packages that your build process creates or needs.

Think of it like a warehouse for your software artifacts.

For example:

Developer
   ↓
GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
Build
   ↓
Nexus Repository
   ↓
Deployment
Why do we use Nexus?

Suppose Jenkins builds your application.

After the build, you may have:

myapp.jar
myapp.war
myapp.zip
Docker image
npm package
Python package

Instead of keeping these files only on the Jenkins server, you can store them in Nexus.

Then other servers or deployment pipelines can download the required version from Nexus.

Example

Jenkins builds:

notes-app-1.0.jar

Jenkins uploads it:

              Nexus
                │
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       │        │        │
    1.0.jar   1.1.jar   2.0.jar

Later, your deployment server can download version 1.1 without rebuilding the application.

Nexus with Docker

This is one of the most useful use cases for you.

Normally you might build:

docker build -t notes-app:latest .

But you don't want the Docker image to exist only on the Jenkins machine.

You can push it to a Nexus Docker registry:

GitHub
   ↓
Jenkins
   ↓
Docker Build
   ↓
notes-app:1.0
   ↓
Nexus Docker Registry

Then your production server can pull:

docker pull nexus-server:8083/notes-app:1.0

So Nexus becomes your private Docker image registry.

Nexus vs GitHub

This is an important difference.

GitHub

Stores your source code:

app.py
index.html
package.json
Dockerfile
Jenkinsfile
Nexus

Stores your built artifacts/packages:

app.jar
app.war
Docker images
npm packages
Maven packages
Python packages

Think:

GitHub = Source Code
Nexus  = Built Software / Packages
Nexus vs Docker Hub

They can serve a similar purpose for Docker images.

Docker Hub

Public/cloud-hosted registry:

Jenkins
   ↓
Docker Hub
   ↓
Production
Nexus

You can run your own private repository:

Jenkins
   ↓
Your Nexus Server
   ↓
Production

This is useful when an organization doesn't want its Docker images or packages publicly available.




